1. Building the system. The circuit uses one Arduino with an HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor (trig/echo on pins 8/7), a photoresistor on analog pin A0, a push button on pin 2 with the internal pull-up enabled (INPUT_PULLUP, so no external resistor is needed), an LED on pin 3, a buzzer on pin 6, and an Adafruit I2C LCD driven over Wire.h. See the pin map below for the full wiring.

2. The state machine. The five required states (OPEN_SEA, ANCHOR_DROPPED, STORM, CHARYBDIS, WRECKED) are modeled as a C enum ShipState, with a single currentState variable driving all behavior. Each loop, the current state is written to the LCD (updateDisplay()), but only when it actually changes, so the screen doesn't flicker on every loop iteration.

3. Anchor (push button). The button is read with a standard debounce routine (50 ms settle time) so a single physical press registers as exactly one state toggle. Pressing it flips a boolean anchorDropped flag: dropping anchor jumps straight to ANCHOR_DROPPED and — critically — the main loop stops calling the sensor-evaluation function entirely while in that state, so the ship truly can't take on storm or Charybdis damage while anchored. Raising the anchor returns to OPEN_SEA and immediately re-checks the sensors in case danger is still present.

4. Wrecked state and the 5-second timer. Entering STORM or CHARYBDIS records a dangerStartTime timestamp. On every subsequent loop, if the danger condition is still true and millis() - dangerStartTime >= 5000, the ship moves to WRECKED. Because dropping anchor switches currentState away from STORM/CHARYBDIS before that check can fire, anchoring within the 5 seconds prevents the wreck — and since a fresh dangerStartTime is only stamped when a danger state is freshly entered, raising the anchor again effectively restarts the timer rather than resuming the old one. Once WRECKED, the loop skips sensor evaluation altogether and the button is explicitly ignored, so nothing can move the ship out of that state short of a reset.

5. Storm (light sensor). stormNow is true whenever the analog light reading drops below LIGHT_THRESHOLD (512 — half of the 0–1023 analog range), which reads as "below half" per the spec. While in STORM, the LED is toggled every 300 ms in updateOutputs(). If the light rises back above the threshold before 5 seconds pass, the state returns to OPEN_SEA.

6. Charybdis (distance sensor). charybdisNow is true whenever readDistanceCm() — which pulses the HC-SR04 and converts echo time to centimeters — reports less than 100 cm. While in CHARYBDIS, the buzzer is held on. If the object moves out of range before 5 seconds pass, the state returns to OPEN_SEA.

7. Simultaneous storm/Charybdis. From OPEN_SEA, if both conditions are true in the same loop iteration, the code checks storm first, so STORM wins that tie. The more important half of the spec's rule — that whichever hazard was entered first keeps its own timer running and isn't interrupted by the other — is handled by having each state's switch case only look at its own trigger condition: once in STORM, the code never checks charybdisNow (and vice versa), so a second hazard appearing mid-timer has no effect on the state or its countdown.
